        "Thirteen companies whose profit is the gap between what they pay for a basic input (benzene, gas, salt, styrene, vinyl chloride, orthoxylene or vegetable-oil waste) and what they can charge for the chemical they make from it, a gap usually set by the price of imports from China, the Gulf and South-East Asia. This is one of five groups on the Chemicals page; the others cover the fluorine chain, custom synthesis and specialty intermediates, carbon, colour and polymer additives, and formulations, ingredients and industrial gases. Three make phenol, ammonia and nitric-acid chains: **Deepak Nitrite**, **Deepak Fertilisers** and **GNFC**. Six live on salt and brine: **Tata Chemicals** and **GHCL** (soda ash), **Archean** (bromine and salt), and **Epigral**, **DCW** and **Chemplast Sanmar** (caustic soda, chlorine and PVC). Four convert petrochemicals: **Supreme Petrochem** (polystyrene), **Styrenix** (ABS plastic), **IG Petrochemicals** (phthalic anhydride) and **Fairchem Organics** (fatty acids from oil-refinery waste). This edition was built from both earnings calls for every company, the FY26 annual reports, rating reports from CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, Acuité and CareEdge, and sector work from Crisil Ratings, ICRA, CareEdge Research and Fitch. The headline: the war in West Asia turned April–June 2026 into a windfall quarter for almost every company here, with margins double or triple normal, and nearly every management said so itself. Crisil expects sector margins to fall back by 1.5–2 points this year, so this note judges each company on normal earnings. The annual reports turned up a ₹21,370cr telecom-licence demand at GNFC that is neither provided for nor shown as a contingent liability, a factory land lease at Archean that expired in 2018, four auditor emphasis matters at DCW, promoter-linked financing at Deepak Fertilisers, an unexplained loss-making Dubai arm at IG Petrochemicals and a debt-funded buyback at Fairchem. Every chart uses filed results.",
