                "Two yardsticks. **P/E** compares the share price with profit after tax. **EV/EBITDA** compares the value of the whole business (market value plus debt, minus cash) with operating profit, so it charges for debt and credits cash. In this group the difference is large, because some companies carry debt that is a large share of their market value and others hold a lot of cash. A second warning: trailing profit includes the Q1 FY27 windfall, so a price that looks cheap on trailing earnings may be fair or expensive on normal ones.",
